From: Aspartame GTK4 migration
Subject: [PATCH] toolbutton: expose the Sugar tooltip as the accessible name

ToolButton.set_tooltip() clears the GTK tooltip on purpose, because Sugar's
palette is the tooltip implementation and a second desktop-themed popup would
be wrong. Nothing then supplied an accessible name, so an icon-only Sugar tool
button reported name='' to assistive technology even though the label text was
sitting right there in the palette.

On Home the two view switchers are exactly that: AT-SPI reported two anonymous
buttons where a user should hear "Favorites view" and "List view". This is
more visible now that focus can reach them.

Set the accessible label from the same string that feeds the palette.

--- a/src/sugar4/graphics/toolbutton.py
+++ b/src/sugar4/graphics/toolbutton.py
@@ -198,6 +198,12 @@
 
         self._tooltip = tooltip
 
+        # The palette replaces the GTK tooltip visually, but assistive
+        # technology still needs the name. Without this an icon-only Sugar
+        # tool button reports an empty accessible name, so Home's view
+        # switchers announced nothing at all.
+        self.update_property([Gtk.AccessibleProperty.LABEL], [tooltip])
+
         if not self.get_palette():
             palette = Palette(tooltip)
             self.set_palette(palette)
